# CLAUDE DESIGN RULES — FOSS INTERACTIVE EXPERIENCE

## 01 — PRIMARY DESIGN PRINCIPLE

The website must NOT look AI-generated.

It must feel like a **professionally art-directed digital experience created by a senior product designer and creative developer**.

Every visual decision must have a reason.

Do not generate UI simply because a UI component is technically convenient.

The design should prioritize:

> **Editorial composition + interaction + typography + spatial depth + motion + visual storytelling**

over:

> cards + gradients + rounded containers + generic component libraries.

---

# 02 — ABSOLUTELY NO GENERIC AI UI

Do NOT create designs that resemble common AI-generated websites.

Avoid:

* Generic SaaS dashboards
* Generic landing pages
* Repeated feature cards
* Three-column card grids
* Excessive rounded rectangles
* Huge gradient blobs
* Purple/blue AI gradients
* Glassmorphism everywhere
* Floating abstract blobs
* Random glowing circles
* Generic statistics sections
* “Trusted by” logo strips
* Fake testimonials
* Generic CTA cards
* Generic pricing-style layouts
* Cookie-cutter hero sections
* Excessive shadows
* Excessive borders
* Excessive pills
* Random icons inside circles
* Stock illustrations
* Generic 3D blobs
* Decorative elements without purpose

If a component could appear unchanged on a random AI SaaS website, **do not use it**.

---

# 03 — NO “CARD EVERYTHING” DESIGN

Do not turn every piece of information into a card.

Cards should only exist when they provide a genuine interaction or grouping purpose.

Bad:

```text
┌────────────┐
│ Git        │
│ Description│
└────────────┘

┌────────────┐
│ Linux      │
│ Description│
└────────────┘

┌────────────┐
│ AI         │
│ Description│
└────────────┘
```

Preferred:

Create a **spatial technology ecosystem** where Git, Linux, AI, Cloud and other technologies exist as connected elements in a larger environment.

The interface should feel like an **environment**, not a dashboard.

---

# 04 — PREMIUM UI PRINCIPLE

Premium design comes from:

* Typography
* Spacing
* Composition
* Restraint
* Motion
* Hierarchy
* Consistency
* High-quality interaction

NOT from:

* More gradients
* More shadows
* More rounded corners
* More animations
* More glowing effects

When uncertain, choose the **simpler and more intentional option**.

---

# 05 — DESIGN WITH COMPOSITION, NOT COMPONENTS

Do not begin every section by asking:

> “Which component should I use?”

Instead ask:

> “What should the audience experience at this moment?”

Each section should have a unique visual composition.

For example:

### FOSS Definition

Don't use five cards.

Instead:

```text
USE
   \
    STUDY
      \
       MODIFY
          \
           SHARE
              \
               CONTRIBUTE
```

Create a visual journey.

---

# 06 — EVERY SECTION NEEDS A VISUAL IDEA

Before implementing a section, define:

1. What is the message?
2. What is the visual metaphor?
3. What is the interaction?
4. What is the transition?
5. What should the audience remember?

If a section only contains:

> Heading + paragraph + cards

it is probably not designed enough.

---

# 07 — USE VISUAL METAPHORS

The website is about FOSS and technology.

Use meaningful visual metaphors such as:

* Git branches
* Contribution graphs
* Networks
* Nodes
* Code
* Terminal environments
* Version history
* Connected systems
* Dependency graphs
* Repository structures
* Pull requests
* Open collaboration
* Digital ecosystems

Avoid meaningless decoration.

Every decorative element should reinforce the concept.

---

# 08 — TYPOGRAPHY IS A PRIMARY DESIGN ELEMENT

Do not treat typography as secondary.

Use typography to create visual drama.

Example:

```text
DON'T JUST

CONSUME

TECHNOLOGY.
```

Then:

```text
SHAPE IT.
```

Large typography can act as part of the visual composition.

Use:

* Large display typography
* Tight hierarchy
* Strong contrast
* Monospace typography for technical elements
* Carefully controlled line lengths

Avoid excessive text.

---

# 09 — DO NOT OVERUSE ROUNDED CORNERS

Rounded corners should not be the default.

Avoid:

```css
border-radius: 24px;
```

on everything.

Use a mixture of:

* Sharp edges
* Slight radius
* Full radius only where interaction requires it

The visual language should feel **technical and architectural**, not like a generic mobile app.

---

# 10 — NO EXCESSIVE GLASSMORPHISM

Glass effects should be extremely limited.

Do NOT make:

* Navigation glass
* Cards glass
* Buttons glass
* Sections glass
* Modals glass
* Backgrounds glass

all at the same time.

If glass is used, it must serve a specific visual purpose.

---

# 11 — COLOR DISCIPLINE

Do not use random colors.

Establish a controlled design system.

Use:

### Base

Deep black / charcoal.

### Primary

Off-white / white.

### Accent

One primary technology accent.

### Secondary

One or two supporting accents only.

Color should communicate:

* interaction
* hierarchy
* state
* technology
* progression

It should not simply make the interface “look cool.”

---

# 12 — NO RANDOM GRADIENTS

Do not add gradients just because the design feels empty.

Bad:

```text
purple → pink → blue → cyan
```

Use gradients only when they create:

* depth
* light
* atmosphere
* focus
* spatial separation

A gradient must have a design purpose.

---

# 13 — 3D MUST HAVE PURPOSE

3D should not exist merely because:

> “The website is supposed to be futuristic.”

Every 3D element must contribute to the story.

Examples:

### Good

A 3D Git ecosystem that evolves as the user moves through the FOSS journey.

### Bad

A random floating 3D sphere in the hero.

### Good

Technology nodes connecting as the concept of collaboration is introduced.

### Bad

Random floating cubes around the page.

---

# 14 — SPLINE IS A DESIGN ELEMENT, NOT A BACKGROUND

If using Spline:

Do not simply place a Spline scene behind text and call the page “3D”.

The webpage should interact visually with the Spline scene.

Possible relationships:

```text
Scroll
 ↓
Spline camera changes
 ↓
Technology nodes activate
 ↓
UI typography changes
 ↓
Next concept appears
```

The 3D environment should feel integrated with the narrative.

---

# 15 — MOTION MUST TELL THE STORY

Never animate an element simply because animation is possible.

Every animation should answer:

> Why is this moving?

Use motion for:

* Progression
* Transformation
* Hierarchy
* Cause and effect
* Spatial relationships
* Focus
* Discovery

Examples:

```text
CONSUMER
   ↓
transforms into
   ↓
EXPLORER
```

This is meaningful motion.

Random floating cards are not.

---

# 16 — AVOID EXCESSIVE MICRO-ANIMATIONS

Do not animate everything.

If every:

* icon
* button
* card
* heading
* paragraph
* background
* particle

is moving simultaneously, the design becomes noisy.

Use **visual silence**.

Some elements should remain completely still.

Contrast creates premium motion.

---

# 17 — USE SCROLL AS A STORYTELLING TOOL

Scrolling should not simply move the page vertically.

Use scroll to control:

* Camera movement
* Typography
* Object positioning
* Technology connections
* Progression
* Transformations
* Scene changes

The user should feel:

> “I am moving through the FOSS ecosystem.”

not:

> “I am scrolling through a website.”

---

# 18 — CREATE DEPTH

Use layers:

```text
BACKGROUND
    ↓
ATMOSPHERE
    ↓
3D ENVIRONMENT
    ↓
STRUCTURAL ELEMENTS
    ↓
TYPOGRAPHY
    ↓
INTERACTION
```

Avoid flat layouts where everything exists on the same visual plane.

---

# 19 — DON'T USE ICONS AS DECORATION

Icons must represent real concepts.

Good:

Git icon → Git section

Terminal icon → developer workflow

Branch icon → contribution flow

Bad:

Random sparkle icon

Random rocket icon

Random lightning icon

Random AI brain icon

Use symbols only when they communicate meaning.

---

# 20 — DESIGN FOR A LARGE SCREEN

This is primarily a **live presentation experience**.

Design for:

* 16:9 screens
* Projectors
* Large monitors
* Webinar screen sharing

Important information should be readable from a distance.

Use:

* Large typography
* Strong contrast
* Simple composition
* Minimal text

Avoid tiny UI.

---

# 21 — NO STOCK PHOTOS

Do not use generic stock photographs unless specifically requested.

The experience should be driven by:

* typography
* 3D
* code
* graphics
* motion
* abstract technology visualization

The visual identity should belong to the FOSS experience.

---

# 22 — NO FAKE CONTENT

Do not invent:

* Statistics
* Member counts
* Achievements
* Testimonials
* Company partnerships
* Event numbers
* GitHub stars
* Contributions
* Awards

If information is not provided, leave it out.

---

# 23 — NO FAKE “TECH COMPANY” LANGUAGE

Avoid meaningless copy such as:

> “Empowering the next generation through innovative solutions.”

> “Unlock limitless possibilities.”

> “Revolutionizing the digital ecosystem.”

Use direct language.

Prefer:

> **Learn how the software works.**

> **Build something real.**

> **Collaborate with others.**

> **Make your first contribution.**

---

# 24 — COPY SHOULD FEEL HUMAN

The tone should be:

* confident
* technical
* curious
* youthful
* direct

Not:

* corporate
* overly inspirational
* generic
* marketing-heavy

---

# 25 — EACH SECTION MUST FEEL DIFFERENT

Do not repeat the same layout.

Avoid:

```text
Heading
Paragraph
3 Cards
Button
```

on every section.

Instead:

### Section 1

Full-screen cinematic hero.

### Section 2

Interactive flow.

### Section 3

Typography transformation.

### Section 4

3D ecosystem.

### Section 5

Visual comparison.

### Section 6

Interactive roadmap.

### Section 7

Contribution ladder.

This creates rhythm.

---

# 26 — USE NEGATIVE SPACE

Do not fill every empty area.

Empty space is intentional.

It creates:

* focus
* elegance
* hierarchy
* anticipation

If a section feels too empty, do not immediately add another card.

First evaluate:

* typography size
* positioning
* scale
* visual balance
* composition

---

# 27 — DESIGN LIKE A CREATIVE DEVELOPER

The implementation should combine:

### Design thinking

What should the user feel?

### Interaction design

What should the user do?

### Motion design

How should the experience evolve?

### Engineering

How can it remain performant?

The goal is not simply:

> “Make a beautiful React page.”

The goal is:

> **Build an interactive visual experience.**

---

# 28 — COMPONENT CREATION RULE

Before creating a reusable component, ask:

1. Does this interaction appear multiple times?
2. Does abstraction improve maintainability?
3. Does the component preserve the visual uniqueness of each section?

Do NOT create generic components such as:

```text
FancyCard
GlassCard
GradientCard
FeatureCard
PremiumCard
AnimatedCard
```

just to make development easier.

Components should represent **real design concepts**.

Good:

```text
TechnologyNode
ContributionLadder
JourneyStage
TerminalSequence
SplineScene
SectionProgress
```

---

# 29 — AVOID DESIGN SYSTEM OVER-ABSTRACTION

Do not force every section into a rigid design system.

A premium editorial experience needs controlled variation.

Maintain consistency through:

* typography
* spacing
* color
* motion language
* interaction principles

not by making every section use the same component.

---

# 30 — DESIGN REVIEW BEFORE CODING

Before implementing any major section, perform a visual review.

Ask:

### Question 1

Could this section appear on a generic AI-generated website?

If YES → redesign.

### Question 2

Does this section communicate a unique idea?

If NO → redesign.

### Question 3

Is the visual metaphor connected to FOSS?

If NO → redesign.

### Question 4

Does the animation have a purpose?

If NO → remove it.

### Question 5

Is the section visually different from the previous section?

If NO → redesign.

---

# 31 — THE “AI SLOP TEST”

Before finalizing the page, inspect the entire experience.

Reject the design if it contains excessive:

* Rounded cards
* Gradients
* Glassmorphism
* Floating blobs
* Generic icons
* Decorative sparkles
* Random 3D objects
* Repeated layouts
* Excessive shadows
* Huge text without purpose
* Generic marketing copy
* Empty sections
* Template-like components

The final result should look like something a **creative technology studio intentionally designed**, not something generated from a generic website prompt.

---

# 32 — PREMIUM DESIGN CHECKLIST

Before considering the implementation complete:

* [ ] Typography feels intentional
* [ ] Spacing feels intentional
* [ ] Every section has a distinct composition
* [ ] No unnecessary cards
* [ ] No generic SaaS patterns
* [ ] No random gradients
* [ ] No excessive glassmorphism
* [ ] No meaningless 3D objects
* [ ] No excessive animation
* [ ] Motion communicates progression
* [ ] 3D supports the narrative
* [ ] Spline is integrated rather than simply embedded
* [ ] Visual hierarchy is obvious
* [ ] Content is concise
* [ ] Large-screen presentation works
* [ ] Mobile experience remains usable
* [ ] Performance is acceptable
* [ ] Accessibility is maintained

---

# 33 — FINAL CREATIVE DIRECTION

The website should feel like:

> **A digital exhibition about open-source technology.**

Not:

> A FOSS club landing page.

It should feel closer to:

**Interactive installation**

*

**Developer environment**

*

**Editorial storytelling**

*

**3D technology visualization**

*

**Premium product design**

The audience should remember the **experience**, not the UI components.

---

# FINAL RULE

### NEVER ASK:

> “What component should I put here?”

### ALWAYS ASK:

> **“What should the audience experience here?”**

Then design the interface around that answer.
